<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wodan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woden was the chief god of the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, corresponding to the Norse Odin and the continental Wodan. Associated with wisdom, war, poetry and the dead, he gave his name to Wednesday. Most Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> royal genealogies, including those of Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, Mercia and Lindsey, traced their kings' descent from him, and his name survives in place names such as Wednesbury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woden.jpg" alt="Woden" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg von Rosen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_von_Rosen_-_Oden_som_vandringsman,_1886_(Odin,_the_Wanderer).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
